        "text": "When pancreatic lipase breaks down triacylglycerols (TAG) into 2-monoacylglycerol (2-MAG) and two free fatty acids (FFAs), the free fatty acids undergo a multi-step journey to be absorbed and utilized"
      }, {
        "text": " by the body.\n\nHere is the step-by-step fate of those free fatty acids:\n\n### 1. Formation"
      }, {
        "text": " of Mixed Micelles (In the Intestinal Lumen)\nFree fatty acids are hydrophobic (water-hating) and"
      }, {
        "text": " cannot easily float through the watery environment of the intestine to reach the cells lining the wall.\n*   **Action:** They associate with"
      }, {
        "text": " **bile salts** (secreted by the liver/gallbladder), along with the 2-MAG, cholesterol, and fat"
      }, {
        "text": "-soluble vitamins.\n*   **Result:** This forms **mixed micelles**. These are tiny, spherical structures with a"
      }, {
        "text": " hydrophilic (water-loving) outer shell and a hydrophobic inner core containing the fatty acids. The micelles act like \"ferry boats"
      }, {
        "text": ",\" transporting the fatty acids across the \"unstirred water layer\" to the surface of the intestinal lining (the brush border).\n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "### 2. Absorption (Entry into the Enterocyte)\nOnce the micelles reach the brush border of the enterocytes"
      }, {
        "text": " (intestinal absorptive cells):\n*   **Action:** The micelles break down, and the free fatty acids detach."
      }, {
        "text": "\n*   **Mechanism:** Because fatty acids are lipids, they can diffuse directly through the lipid bilayer of the cell membrane"
      }, {
        "text": ". There are also specific transport proteins (like FATP and CD36) that facilitate this uptake. The bile salts remain in"
      }, {
        "text": " the lumen to be reabsorbed further down the intestine (in the ileum).\n\n### 3. Re-ester"
      }, {
        "text": "ification (Inside the Cell)\nThis is a critical step. Once inside the enterocyte, the body does not want the fatty"
      }, {
        "text": " acids to leak back out or disrupt cell membranes.\n*   **Action:** The free fatty acids are transported to the smooth"
      }, {
        "text": " endoplasmic reticulum (SER).\n*   **Result:** Enzymes re-attach the free fatty acids to the 2-MAG"
      }, {
        "text": " backbone. This reverses the digestion process, turning them **back into Triacylglycerols (TAGs)**.\n\n### 4. Packaging"
      }, {
        "text": " into Chylomicrons\nThe newly re-synthesized TAGs are too large and oily to enter the bloodstream directly.\n*"
      }, {
        "text": "   **Action:** The Golgi apparatus packages the TAGs with cholesterol, phospholipids, and a specific protein called **Apol"
      }, {
        "text": "ipoprotein B-48**.\n*   **Result:** This creates a lipoprotein particle called a **chylomicron**."
      }, {
        "text": "\n\n### 5. Transport to Circulation\n*   **Action:** The chylomicrons are released from the enterocyte via ex"
      }, {
        "text": "ocytosis.\n*   **Destination:** Unlike amino acids and sugars, which enter the blood, chylomicrons are too big"
      }, {
        "text": " to enter the capillaries. Instead, they enter the **lacteals** (lymphatic vessels) located inside the intestinal"
      }, {
        "text": " villi.\n*   **Final Fate:** They travel through the lymphatic system and eventually drain into the bloodstream near the heart (via"
      }, {
        "text": " the thoracic duct), where they can finally deliver fats to tissues like muscle and adipose tissue.\n\n***\n\n**Important Exception ("
      }, {
        "text": "Short- \u0026 Medium-Chain Fatty Acids):**\nIf the free fatty acids released are *short-chain* or *"
      }, {
        "text": "medium-chain* (fewer than 10-12 carbons), they do **not** require micelles or re-"
      }, {
        "text": "esterification. They pass directly through the enterocyte and enter the portal vein blood, going straight to the liver."
